1. What is HashMap?
Hashmap is data structure that work on key <---> value mapping

2. When & Why do we use it?
 we need mapping type data structure
for example any object map with some data
ex:- prices of different object
ex:- frequency mapping of object

3. Usage through collections framework and Examples.
Map Interface have  Hashmap class

4. Working with HashMaps/Important Methods
1.Syntax
2.Adding Element
get ---> Value, if key exist
    ---> null , if Key doesn't exit

put  ---> Intersection , if Key doesn't exits
     ---> update  , if key already exits

remove ---> Value , if key exits
        ---> null , if key doesn't exits

contains ---> true , if key exits
         ---> false , if key not exits

==> Important feature of HashMap Class
1. To access a value one must know its key.
2. HashMap doesn't allow duplicate keys but allow duplicate values.This means
   a single key can't have more than one value, but multiple keys can have
   the same value..
3. HashMap allows null key also but only once and multiple null values
4. Java HashMaps maintains no order

===> Applications of HashMaps
1. Problems related to frequency of an item
2. Mapping problems
3. Storage optimization
4. Dictionary
5. Phonebook

5.How/Internal working - Hashing/Hash function / collisions
Hashing - arbitrary(random) sized key convert into smaller size key(Hashed key)
by passing Hashed Function

What are Hash Functions?
The function which are use for hashing that is knows as Hash Functions

The hash function should give the same output for the same input
  H(ip1)  = op1
  H(ip1)  = op1

  A perfect hash function always produces unique for unique input

  A non-perfect hash function can give the same output for different inputs.
  This is known as a collision.

What is Hashing?

Key  --> hash f(x) --> hash value(index) --> put in HashTable

Generally used Hash Functions
1. Division method
h(k) = k mod M

2. Mid square Method
h(k) = h(k*k)

3. Digit Folding Method
k = k1,k2,k3,k4 ... , kn  -- Divide into segments to equal size
s =k1+k2+k3+k4+...+kn     -- Take sum  --> s
h(k)=s                    -- S % M

4. Multiplication Method
h(k) = floor(M(K*A mod 1))

Collisions
Sometimes , Hash function can result in same hash value for different
keys.

k % M  M =9
 k = 20 % 9 = 2
 k = 29 % 9 = 2
 k = 38 % 9 = 2

so how we can put this in key - value in same index of table this is pro

this is solved by LinkedList implementation

put --> Insert (key insert)
        Update(key exits)

Hash function  --> bucketIndex(bi)
buckets[bi]   ---> Traverse this LL & See if key already exits.
                    if Yes: update the value in nodes
                    else : Add a New Node.

but this is also problem because longest length of linked list
so time complexity will increase

solution -> Good Hash Functions
            More brackets / capacity (Usually double)
            * Load Factor -> Measure the decides when
                             to double array size
             Default LF --> 75% of capacity

             lembda --> LF / size

   Threshold value = Load Factor * Capacity
                   =   0.75 * 100
                   =  75
Conclusion: when the number of entries in the map crosses the threshold value

      A new bucket array of double the size of previous bucket array
      is created all the entries are copied from old array to new array

Summary:- 1) Array of LinkedList <Node>
          2) Put , Get , Remove
          3) Load Factor: 0.75 (l)
          4) Threshold value: l * N(capacity)
          5) Rehashing

Q. Given a hash table T with 25 slots that stores 2000 elements ,
   What is the load factor @ for T?

   T =  n / N  where n = elements , N = Slots

Open Addressing [ Closed hashing ].
Linear probing
where i is prob number --> No of Attempts
(H(k) + i ) % M   ---> where i is the index which check linearly is index
                       empty or not


Quadratic
(H(k) + i^2 ) % M    ----> i will be quadratic

